// The half `0.9.38` could not reach: a receiver whose type MENTIONS the type parameter. An opaque type
// parameter makes `DynamicArray<T>` an ordinary instance, so an unknown method on it is as wrong here as
// it is on `DynamicArray<int32>` — and it is wrong at EVERY instantiation, which is why the template
// itself is the right place to say so rather than each consumer's use site.
//
// Not hypothetical: `tests/generic_uninst_ok.kama` carried exactly this call, in the fixture built to
// assert that valid generic code is left alone, and nothing saw it until this pass existed.
import { std::collections::DynamicArray };

fn void neverCalled<T>(ref DynamicArray<T> src) {
    T item = src.get(index: 0);          // ERROR: `DynamicArray` has no `get`
}
fn int32 main() { return 0; }
